   "source": [
    "# Count transactions from each origin facility\n",
    "origin_count = df_filtered[\"Origin_Facility\"].value_counts()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Create bar chart\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
    "origin_count.plot(kind=\"bar\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Add the title and axis labels\n",
    "plt.title(\"Number of Transactions by Origin Facility\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Origin Facility\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Number of Transactions\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Rotate the facility names to make them easier to read\n",
    "plt.xticks(rotation=45, ha=\"right\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Display bar chart\n",
    "plt.show()\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5f222333-b6d6-40ce-8014-db8cef270471",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Conclusion:\n",
    "We agree with Bob because there are 8,905 unique destination cities in the filtered dataset. If we were to create a bar chart with this many categories, the graph would be very overcrowded and difficult to read and interpret. As a result, we would not be able to easily compare the number of transactions between different cities. Instead of creating a bar chart, we could use a different visual representation or group the transactions by geographical area to make the data easier to read and interpret. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "60678bb9-1661-40e6-b263-cb8dcab7280f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Question 5"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 24,
   "id": "66497b53-e31b-42fa-a447-4f8ed37f9f96",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1000x600 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "\n",
    "# Create scatter plot\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
    "\n",
    "# Loop through each traffic condition\n",
    "for traffic in df_filtered[\"Traffic_Conditions\"].unique():\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Filter the dataset for each traffic condition\n",
    "    data = df_filtered[df_filtered[\"Traffic_Conditions\"] == traffic]\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Plot the distance against CO2 emissions\n",
    "    plt.scatter(data[\"Distance_KM\"],\n",
    "                data[\"Carbon_Emission_kgCO2e\"],\n",
    "                label=traffic,\n",
    "                alpha=0.5,\n",
    "                s=10)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Add the title and axis labels\n",
    "plt.title(\"CO2 Emissions vs Distance by Traffic Conditions\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Distance (KM)\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"CO2 Emissions (kgCO2e)\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Add a legend to distinguish between the different traffic conditions\n",
    "plt.legend(title=\"Traffic Conditions\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Display the scatter plot\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9a27007e-d979-4409-a1cd-c3627e621ce7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Conclusion: \n",
    "The scatter plot shows a positive correlation between distance and CO2 emissions, meaning that longer delivery distances are associated with higher CO2 emissions. It also shows that there is a positive correlation between traffic congestion and CO2 emissions, as the heavier traffic conditions are associated with higher emissions."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "684e26f9-1def-4e97-be08-a55647ba0bff",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Question 6"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 25,
   "id": "01a3e3f0-a0cd-4cf3-a956-27edc4904843",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1000x600 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Create scatter plot\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
    "\n",
    "#Plot the package weight against CO2 emissions\n",
    "plt.scatter(df_filtered[\"Package_Weight_KG\"],\n",
    "            df_filtered[\"Carbon_Emission_kgCO2e\"],\n",
    "            alpha=0.5,\n",
    "            s=10, c = \"red\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Add the title and axis labels\n",
    "plt.title(\"CO2 Emissions vs Package Weight\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Package Weight (KG)\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"CO2 Emissions (kgCO2e)\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Display the scatter plot\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4a9fa4ee-66c7-45b6-bf6a-473bb1b1b067",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Conclusion: \n",
    "The scatter plot shows no observable correlation between package weight and CO2 emissions, as emissions remain relatively similar across different package weights. There is also a cluster of packages with weights close to 0 kilograms which generally have lower CO2 emissions."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "418d5bf7-e899-483a-b865-bfc414864128",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Question 7"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d11a9fa9-5d1f-4125-9ce7-77f07aace418",
   "metadata": {
    "jp-MarkdownHeadingCollapsed": true
   },
   "source": [
    "### Variables:\n",
    "Our dependent variable is CO2 emissions, and the potential independent variables we can choose between are distance travelled, vehicle type, route type, traffic conditions, eco-friendliness, and package weight. For our analysis, the specific independent variables we would like to choose are distance travelled and vehicle type. We chose these two variables as distance showed a positive correlation with CO2 emissions in Question 5, while we believe that different vehicle types may also produce varying amounts of emissions. In our analysis, we expect a positive correlation between distance travelled and CO2 emissions, as well as varying CO2 emissions among the different vehicle types. \n",
    "### Challenges:\n",
    "One challenge with these independent variables is that they include both numerical and categorical data. Vehicle type is categorical, meaning we would need to convert the data into numerical variables before using it in a regression model. An additional challenge is that distance and vehicle type may be related, with certain vehicles being used for the longer distance deliveries, making it potentially difficult to determine how much each variable individually contributes to the CO2 emissions."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d43cf827-60eb-4662-b2f9-8e8cfcfd0d79",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Question 8"
   ]
  },
