   "source": [
    "import statsmodels.formula.api as smf\n",
    "\n",
    "# Create multiple linear regression model using distance and vehicle type\n",
    "model = smf.ols(\n",
    "    \"Carbon_Emission_kgCO2e ~ Distance_KM + C(Vehicle_Type)\",\n",
    "    data=df_filtered\n",
    ").fit()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Display regression results\n",
    "print(model.summary())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "332d8e64-a668-4b96-bfbf-232fbddeedee",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Conclusion:\n",
    "The regression model has an R-squared value of 0.942, meaning that the independent variables in the model (distance and vehicle type) explain 94.2% of the variation in the dependent variable (CO2 emissions). The coefficient for distance is positive (0.928), showing that longer distances are associated with higher CO2 emissions. This variable is also statistically significant, which can be observed by looking at the p-value. For vehicle type, Heavy Trucks have the highest positive coefficient (54.2581), while Electric Vans have the most negative coefficient (-61.6137), both compared to Cargo Bicycles (the reference category) at the same distance. Vehicle types with positive coefficients indicate higher predicted CO2 emissions when compared to the reference category, while vehicle types with negative coefficients indicate lower predicted CO2 emissions when holding the distance travelled constant. Additionally, all vehicle types have statistically significant coefficients, except for Drone Delivery, which has a p-value of 0.886. Overall, the results show that both the distance travelled and vehicle type are important factors associated with CO2 emissions, as was expected in our hypothesis."
   ]
  }
